.TH LORE 1 "2026-11-23" "lore 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lore \- every command you can type, and what each one does, offline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lore
.br
.B lore
.I NAME
.br
.B lore
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lore
lists every command you can type, each name once, sorted A to Z under
section letters (names that start with a digit or a symbol go under
.BR # ).
It needs no internet.
.PP
The list holds the programs on your
.B $PATH
and in the places a
.B $PATH
often leaves out (/usr/sbin, Homebrew, MacPorts, Nix, Snap, Flatpak,
~/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, ~/go/bin, /opt/*/bin and, on macOS, the directories
in /etc/paths), the builtins and keywords of your shell (bash, zsh or fish,
from
.BR $SHELL ),
and your aliases, each shown with what it runs.
.PP
A command's page shows, top to bottom: what it is and does; VERSION, its
package, the installed and the newest known version and how to update it,
read offline from the package manager (dpkg and apt, rpm, pacman or
Homebrew's folders); HOW TO USE, its most common uses; NOTES; LINKS, to its
own site and to Stack Overflow, GeeksforGeeks and Google searches; and its
manual page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I NAME
Open
.IR NAME 's
page straight away, as
.B man
.I NAME
would. Closing the page quits. A name not found on this machine still
opens if lore or
.B man
has a page for it.
.TP
.I "PROGRAM SUBCOMMAND"
Open a subcommand's page, such as
.BR "lore git commit" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage text.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print lore's version.
.SH KEYS
.SS In the list
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through the commands; the letter headers are skipped.
.TP
.B Right, Enter
Open the selected command's page.
.TP
.B /
Go to the search bar. Typing keeps the names that contain the text,
ignoring case, ranked best first instead of A to Z: the exact name, then
names starting with the text (shorter first), then names with it at the
start of a word (git rm), then anywhere. From three characters on, the
pages are searched too, by their categories, summary and examples, and
their matches follow: "compress folder" lists tar and zip, and a command
named by one of the words ("cut files") comes first among them. Words
match at the start of words ("cut" finds "cuts", not "execute"), and a
plural also finds its singular. Each result shows, after its name, the
page line that matched, cut around the match
.RB ( "[Cut out fields from stdin or files.]" ),
then its categories. With the search empty, the list is A to Z again. Common words count as their usual equivalents
(folder as directory, delete as remove). Start
with
.B @
to keep only the commands that have a lore page
.RB ( @gr ).
Esc leaves the search bar and keeps its text; Backspace erases it.
.TP
.B Esc
Quit.
.SS On a page
.TP
.B Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn
Scroll.
.TP
.B /
Search other commands without leaving the page: the list beside it
filters as you type, and Enter or Right opens the chosen command's page in
place. Esc leaves the search for the full list.
.TP
.B Left, Esc
Back to the list, or quit when the page was opened as
.B lore
.IR NAME .
.SS Anywhere
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Quit.
.SH CATEGORIES AND DANGER
In the list, each command is followed by its categories, such as
.BR "[archives files]" .
.B danger
marks a command that can destroy data or stop the system; its page warns
under the summary, and every example that can is marked with
.BR ! .
.SH LANGUAGES
Pages are shown in the language of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.B LC_MESSAGES
or
.B LANG
when lore has a translation (from tldr-pages, 41 languages); otherwise in
English.
.SH LINKS
Every URL is shown whole, on a line of its own, so the terminal can open
it: Ctrl+click in Konsole and most terminals. lore never opens a link
itself, shows only https links, and never asks you to log in anywhere or
for any account. Check a link before you open it: anyone can add pages.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lore/content/NAME.md
.TQ
.I /usr/local/share/lore/content/NAME.md
.TQ
.I /usr/share/lore/content/NAME.md
A page for
.IR NAME ,
checked in this order before the pages built into lore; the first one
found is shown. None of these folders needs to exist: lore carries its own
pages. The format is in content/README.md in lore's repository.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal quit; 1 when there is no terminal to draw on (a pipe, or
.B ssh
without
.BR \-t ),
on an unknown option, or when
.I NAME
has no command, lore page or manual page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR whatis (1),
.BR apropos (1)
.PP
https://github.com/guhhammer/lore
